  "Number": 18,
  "Question": "A right triangle has perimeter \\(32\\) and area \\(20.\\) What is the length of its hypotenuse?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{57}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{59}{4}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{61}{4}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{63}{4}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{65}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Let the positive leg lengths be \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), and let the hypotenuse be \\(c\\). The area gives \\(ab/2=20\\), so \\(ab=40\\), while the perimeter gives \\(a+b=32-c\\).\n\nWe can find \\(c\\) without finding the individual legs: squaring their sum brings in both their product and the Pythagorean relation \\(a^2+b^2=c^2\\). Thus\n\\[\n(32-c)^2=(a+b)^2=a^2+b^2+2ab=c^2+80.\n\\]\nExpanding and canceling \\(c^2\\) leaves\n\\[\n1024-64c=80,\n\\qquad c=\\frac{944}{64}=\\frac{59}{4}.\n\\]\nThe hypotenuse has length \\(\\boxed{\\frac{59}{4}}\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
